it is empty, Curvine derives it from `journal.journal_addrs` using `master.rpc_port`; if `journal_addrs` is also empty, it uses `master.hostname:master.rpc_port`.","locale":"en"},"883":{"id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf-36","page_id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf#how-the-config-is-loaded","content":"The standalone Transfer service uses a reduced loader (`ClusterConf::from_transfer`) that validates only the client and transfer sections.","locale":"en"},"884":{"id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf-37","page_id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf#net_interface","content":"When `net_interface` is non-empty (for example `\"eth0\"`), Curvine resolves the first IPv4 address of that interface and applies it to the `master`, `mds`, `journal`, `worker`, `client`, and `transfer` hostnames. When it is set, the `CURVINE_*_HOSTNAME` environment variables are ignored.","locale":"en"},"885":{"id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf-38","page_id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf#net_interface","content":"`net_interface` only fills in **this node's own** hostname. Cluster membership lists (`journal.journal_addrs` and `client.master_addrs`) must still be written explicitly, and each node's entry must use the exact IPv4 that its interface resolves to. Otherwise the node cannot locate itself in the Raft group, and startup fails with a clear error.","locale":"en"},"886":{"id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf-39","page_id":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Cluster/Distributed-Mode/conf","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Deploy/Deploy-Curvine-Clu